// UsingInstances.fol — application entry file
// Spec: language-ref.md > Using an Instance, Where an Instance Is Declared,
//       Activating Instance Methods
// Grammar: use-directive, use-field (DECISION-SEM-001, DECISION-SEM-002)
//
// An instance is selected BY NAME. FoLang never searches visible packages for
// an instance that happens to match a type, so nothing is inferred and an
// unresolved call names something the developer actually wrote.

@co.ddap.import(package="abc.tc", as="tc")

xs List(co.lang.int) = [1, 2, 3];

// An entry file cannot declare a function, so the mapped operation is written
// as a closure declaration — see 07-functions for the full form.
double = (x co.lang.int) ==>> x * 2;

// --- calling an instance by name -----------------------------------------
// No activation needed. `map` takes the container as its first argument,
// exactly as the typeclass declares it, and the call names the instance the
// same way it names any other imported declaration.
ys := tc.ListFunctor.map(xs, double);

// --- activating instance methods -----------------------------------------
// Activation is what lets the same function be called as a method. It is
// explicit and block-scoped, so importing a package activates nothing and can
// never change how an existing call resolves.
@co.ddap.use(from="tc.ListFunctor", methods=[map, reduce])

zs := xs.map(double);        // resolves to tc.ListFunctor.map(xs, double)

// --- the field list is closed (DECISION-SEM-002) --------------------------
// "from" and "methods" are the only keys, matching import-field, so a mistyped
// key is a parse error rather than an argument that is silently ignored:
//
//     @co.ddap.use(from="tc.ListFunctor", method=[map])    // ERR unknown field
//     @co.ddap.use(from="tc.ListFunctor", extensions=[map]) // ERR no such key
//
// There is no "extensions" key. An extension already declares what it extends
// through @co.dap.extension on the method itself, so the activation site has
// nothing to restate — which is why one key covers both sources:
//
//     @co.ddap.use(from="tu.stringextension", methods=[upperCase])  // extension unit
//     @co.ddap.use(from="tc.ListFunctor", methods=[map, reduce])    // instance
//
// Listing names is optional. Omit the list to activate everything the source
// provides; give a list to activate a subset. A subset is how conflicts are
// resolved — take `map` from one instance and `reduce` from another.

// --- what "from" accepts --------------------------------------------------
// "from" names a DECLARATION, deliberately unlike "package", which names a
// package and nothing else:
//
//     from="stringextension"           bare — same package
//     from="tc.ListFunctor"            alias + instance
//     from="ext.stringextension"       alias + unit
//     from="abc.tc.ListFunctor"        full package + instance

// --- resolution order (semantic) ------------------------------------------
// For xs.map(f), where xs has type List(A), the compiler tries in order:
//
//   1. a class method or companion-unit function on List
//   2. an activated extension for List
//   3. an activated instance function whose typeclass declares map with the
//      receiver as its first parameter
//   4. otherwise, an error
//
// First match wins, so a type's own declarations always outrank anything
// activated into scope. Within one scope a method name may be activated at
// most once per receiver type; a second activation is an error naming both
// sources, reported where the activation is written rather than at a call.
//
// Method syntax is unrelated to typeclasses on its own: xs.map(f) may equally
// call an associated function from a companion unit. A companion function and
// an instance function may both be named map; they are different declarations
// reached in the order above.

// --- where an instance is declared (semantic) -----------------------------
// An instance is declared in the package defining the TYPECLASS or the package
// defining the TYPE — that exact package, not a sub-package:
//
//     abc.tc.ListFunctor      for=Functor, type=List           OK  typeclass's package
//     myapp.ab.TreeFunctor    for=Functor, type=myapp.ab.Tree  OK  type's package
//     other.util.ListFunctor  for=Functor, type=List           ERR neither is theirs
//
// The rule is permissive on both sides on purpose: requiring the typeclass's
// package alone would make a typeclass usable only by its author, and requiring
// the type's package alone would stop a library shipping instances for built-in
// types. If you need an instance for a typeclass and a type you both do not
// own, wrap the type in a co.lang.newtype you do own.
//
// This placement rule is semantic, not syntactic. A misplaced instance PARSES
// correctly and is reported during name resolution, so the diagnostic can name
// the typeclass, the type, and the two packages that would have been legal.
